d reference loggers directly targets the mandate to technically allow automatic recording of relevant events.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms... Open source benchmark, collaboration. Acceptable. 4.\n\nEvidence: Publishing an open-source logging schema and collaborating with peers is a common, norm-compliant research practice.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy low cost or non-radical changes. Proposal requires working group of three researchers one quarter; two engineer-adjacent staff build reference loggers for at least four categories; invite three labs. That's some changes, reasonably feasible but not trivial low cost? Might be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Could be 3.\n\nEvidence: It requires dedicated researcher time, engineering effort, and multi-lab coordination over several months.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree rare ingenious imaginative surprising.\nProposal is open benchmark for logging taxonomy. Is it unusual/interesting? Probably 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Not totally not expressed before? Might be 3. Could be 2 interesting. I'd say 3.\n\nEvidence: An open collaborative benchmark with quantitative metrics for log adequacy is unusual in compliance-driven R&D contexts.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree preserves/modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving\n\nProposal positions lab at intellectual centre of post-market-monitoring research while solving engineering problem. It changes 