        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Need rating.\n\nProposal P2184: Formalising Substantial-Modification Detection for Living Models. Theoretical and applied project to define detect certify when production AI system has undergone change large enough to constitute 'substantial modification' under Article 12(2)(a), triggering renewed logging obligations. Build on existing model-drift-detection work to formalise thresholds... stress-test across three production pipelines... turns vague regulatory trigger into precise publishable decision procedure...\n\nRate six subscales.\n\nImplementability: degree easily implemented. It builds on existing model-drift detection work, formalise thresholds, stress-test across three pipelines. Some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Probably level 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Evidence: leverages existing drift detection and limited to three pipelines.\n\nScore 3?\n\nAcceptability: socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies that violate no norms? Formalising detection is common compliance strategy, does not offend. Level 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities. Maybe 4.\n\nEffectiveness: degree will solve problem. Problem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Proposal addresses substantial modification detection triggering renewed logging obligations. Is that solving main problem? Article 12 requires logging capabilities enable recording events relevant for identifying situations that may result in risk or substantial modification. Detecting substantial modification helps know when logging obligations renew, but does it solve logging mandate? Partial contribution. Reasonable and will contribute to solution, not fully solve logging. So effectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial).\n\nApplicability: degree clearly applies to stated problem. Solves identified problem directly related? Logging mandate includes identifying substantial modification situations. Detecting substantial modification is directly related to Article12(2)(a). So solves identified problem directly related. Level 4.\n\nOriginality: degree rare ingenious... Formalising substantial modification detection using drift metrics. Unusual, interesting shows some imagination? Might be unusual interesting. Not expressed before AND ingenious? Possibly paradigm stretching. Could be originality 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Not necessarily ingenious surprising. I'd say 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree preserves/modifies paradigm. Proposal introduces new elements and changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations? It turns vague regulatory trigger into precise publishable decision procedure that strengthens both compliance posture and broader ML-systems literature on versioned auditable deployments. That is paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance). Or slightly paradigm stretching? Introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice? It formalises detection, maybe still logging practice. Could be 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (logging woven into science). It says strengthens both compliance posture and broader ML literature. That suggests change relationship.\n\nSo paradigm_relatedness 3.\n\nNow produce output with evidence sentence then score.\n\nNeed format: for each subscale in order listed: implementability, acceptability, effectiveness, applicability, originality, paradigm_relatedness\n\nGive \"<subscale>_evidence\" (one sentence) first and then \"<subscale>\" (integer).\n\nProbably JSON? It says return rating echoing its id exactly. Provide maybe lines.\n\nWill output:\n\nid: P2184\nimplementability_evidence: ...\nimplementability: 3\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence each.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\nImplementability evidence: Building on existing model-drift-detection work and testing only three production pipelines makes formalising thresholds reasonably feasible with limited engineering effort.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Defining objective thresholds for substantial modification aligns with regulatory intent and does not violate legal norms or business sensibilities.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: A precise detection procedure will help identify when renewed logging obligations trigger, contributing to traceability but not by itself implementing the required logs.\n\nApplicability evidence: The project directly targets Article 12(2)(a)'s requirement to record events relevant for identifying substantial modification, which is part of the stated logging mandate.\n\nOriginality evidence: Translating a vague legal trigger into formal feature-space and output-distribution thresholds is unusual for an R&D lab and shows imagination beyond standard compliance checklists.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: The work weaves compliance detection into core model versioning research and aims for publishable ML-systems contributions, changing how R&D and compliance obligations relate.\n\nScores: implementability 3, acceptability 4, effectiveness 3, applicability 4, originality 3, paradigm_relatedness 3.\n\nReturn.",
