rifier identities at rest, limits retention... Might still be acceptable? Could be reasonable.\n\nMaybe effectiveness = 3: contributes to solution (manage GDPR risk) but doesn't fully ensure compliance with Article 12 identification requirement.\n\nImplementability: degree to which idea can be easily implemented. Legal and DPO teams will draft minimisation rule... Some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. That's level 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Not easy low cost? Probably 3.\n\nAcceptability: degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies that violate no norms... It's GDPR aligned, so acceptable. Level 4 = common strategies that violate no norms.\n\nThus ratings:\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ... score 2\noriginality_evidence: ... score 1\napplicability_evidence: ... score 4\neffectiveness_evidence: ... score 3? Might be 3.\n\nLet's decide effectiveness: proposal aims to ensure logging does not create GDPR exposure, but pseudonymising verifier identities may conflict with Article 12(3)(d) requirement to record identification. So it will not fully solve logging mandate; it's partial and potentially non-compliant. Could be 2 unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem. The anchor for 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). Would pseudonymisation solve main problem? No, main problem is meet logging mandate. Pseudonymisation may undermine traceability. So not solve.\n\nI think effectiveness = 2 unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem. Hmm but it's reasonable contribution to GDPR compliance. Could be 3 contribute.\n\nGiven the instrument says rate each subscale on its own evidence: proposal that is hard to implement is NOT therefore less relevant. Effectiveness is about solving problem. The stated problem is meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Proposal does adapt R&D to avoid GDPR exposure, which is part of adapting.