nce: If implementable, quarterly log queries and annual reports would provide continuous traceability and meet Article 12 and 72 requirements.\n\nScore 4.\n\nNovelty paradigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving\n\nProposal converts regulatory obligation into unique longitudinal dataset and pursues peer-reviewed papers on long-horizon reliability. That's changing relationship between R&D and compliance obligations. Might be paradigm stretching? It changes relationship: logging woven into science. Paradigm breaking requires introduces new elements AND changes relationship... or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. It does introduce new elements (multi-year programme, internal analytics) and changes relationship. Could be 4 paradigm breaking.\n\nEvidence: The proposal reframes mandatory logging from a compliance cost into the core research object, altering how R&D is driven by regulatory data.\n\nScore 4? Maybe 3? Let's interpret. Paradigm breaking introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. It does both. I'd say 4.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but ingenious... 4 = not expressed before (rare, unusual) AND ingenious, imaginative, or surprising. 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. 2 = interesting. 1 = common.\n\nIs using logs for longitudinal studies rare? Might be unusual. Could be ingenious. Probably 3 or 4. Reference c