ion and automatic recording logs. Article 12 is logging. The proposal explicitly references Recital 71 requirement for up-to-date technical documentation. So it's about record-keeping broadly. Might be considered directly related.\n\nI think applicability =3: solves an implied problem that is related to stated problem (do X to get implied Y which applies). It does documentation refresh, which helps compliance generally.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree will solve problem.\nWill it solve logging mandate? No, it addresses documentation not logs. So partial. Effectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps but only partial). Might be 3.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): socially legally politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies violate no norms. This is standard process improvement. Acceptability 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented.\nSome changes, reasonably feasible measures. Assign one engineer per sprint to update docs through code-review pipeline, QA blocks release. That's some change but feasible. Could be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Or 4 easy low cost? Requires process change and blocking releases maybe moderate. I'd say 3.\n\nProvide output with subscale evidence then score.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale in order listed: FIRST write one sentence of evidence for that subscale (your reasoning before the number). THEN give its score as integer level.\n\nProbably output like:\nid P2993\noriginality_evidence: ...\noriginality: 2\n...\n\nEnsure echo id exactly.\n\nReturn rating.\n\nLet's produce concise sentences.\n\nOriginality evidence: The sprint-embedded documentation update is a recognizable DevOps practice applied to AI compliance and lacks surprising ingenuity. Score 2.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: It introduces a process tool for living documentation but keeps compliance as a separate logging/documentation practice rather than changing R&D-compliance relationship. Score 2.\n\nAppl