s AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving\n\nProposal turns compliance cost into publishable contribution, weaves logging into science. That's paradigm stretching: changes relationship between R&D and compliance obligations. Not necessarily breaking? It changes relationship, maybe stretching. Could be 3. It doesn't address larger problem of what lab researches? It does make logging a research topic. Might be paradigm stretching. I think 3.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree rare but ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane\n\nEfficient lifetime logging as research workstream is unusual? Many labs do compliance research. Might be interesting but not highly original. Probably 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Could be 2 = interesting. Let's pick 3.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree will solve problem.\nProblem: must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nWill this solve? It investigates compression etc., validated against thresholds. If successful would reduce operational expense. But it's a research track for 18 months; may not guarantee meeting mandate immediately. It's reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial). So 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). Could be 4? It wouldn't solve without regard for workability? It would solve if you could do it? Might still need actual logging implementation. It's a research track not immediate implementation. So 3.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\nProposal directly addresses logging mandate and storage/compute cost. So 4.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably JSON? The instruction says return rating... For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Likely produce lines.\n\nLet's output as:\n\nid: P2087\nimplementability_evidence: ...\nimplementability: 3\n...\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nMake sure evidence sentence ends with period.\n\nProvide final.\n\n",
