irectly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\nProblem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Shared middleware directly addresses logging mandate. So 4.\n\nEffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\nWill capture logs required by Article 12... Should solve main problem of compliance logging overhead. Probably 4? It will solve logging mandate. Might be partial? It addresses logging but not adaptation? Problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. It meets logging mandate. So 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem.\n\nNovelty paradigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance...\n\nShared middleware is a tool/process for compliance. Still treats compliance as logging practice. Introduces new elements (reusable SDK). That's slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. Could be 2.\n\nOriginality: degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nShared logging middleware is fairly common engineering practice. Common, mundane. So 1.\n\nLet's ensure anchored levels exactly.\n\nAcceptability evidence sentence: The proposal uses standard internal tooling to meet a legal requirement and does not conflict w