gging mandate? It provides a systematic allocation method, which addresses resource commitment decision. Could be considered solves problem of deciding how to respond with proportional cost. Might be 4? The stated problem: must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources.\n\nTiered approach gives decision framework. It will solve the allocation problem, but still requires actual logging implementation. Probably contributes partially. I'd say 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial solution). Because you still need to actually implement logs.\n\nEvidence: Tiering guides resource allocation for logging but does not itself build the logging capabilities required by Article 12.\n\nScore 3.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem that is directly related...\n3 = solves implied problem that is related...\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation...\n1 = intervention not stated...\n\nProposal directly addresses how to meet logging mandate with scarce resources. That's directly related. Score 4.\n\nEvidence: The memo explicitly targets the Article 12 logging mandate and resource allocation for compliance.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change...\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nIssuing internal memo, assigning tiers at kickoff, tying engineering allocation. That's relatively easy, low cost administrative change. Score 4.\n\nEvidence: Creating a memo and a kickoff checklist requires only policy work and no new technology.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, or politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\n2 = offend