aceability and post-market monitoring, but does it solve logging mandate? Logging mandate is to record events; dashboard uses logs but doesn't create logs. Might contribute. The problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Dashboard is downstream of logging. Might be partial solution. Effectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial). Could be 4? It won't solve logging mandate itself, just use logs. So contribute.\n\nOriginality: degree rare but ingenious. Post-market dashboard on legacy servers. Is it common? Using idle servers is somewhat unusual but not ingenious. Might be interesting. Level 2 = interesting. Or 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Repurposing idle GPU servers for compliance dashboard is unusual. Maybe 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: paradigm preserving vs stretching. Introduces new elements (dashboard) but still treats compliance as logging practice. That's slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice = level 2. Could be 2. Not changing relationship between R&D and compliance obligations fundamentally? It closes loop. Might be slightly stretching. I'll give 2.\n\nNeed output format: echo id, for each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nProvide one sentence evidence first then score.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P1529\n\nimplementability_evidence: ...\nimplementability: 4\n\nacceptability_evidence...\nacceptability:4\n\napplicability_evidence...\napplicability:4\n\neffectiveness_evidence...\neffectiveness:3\n\noriginality_evidence...\noriginality:3? Choose 3.\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence...\nparadigm_relatedness:2\n\nEnsure one sentence each.\n\nLet's produce final.",
