p (e.g., logging woven into the science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nThis is embedding compliance at infrastructure level prevents retrofitting... signals traceability as engineering constraint rather than documentation afterthought. This seems slightly paradigm stretching? Introduces new elements (middleware) but still treats compliance as logging practice. It changes relationship somewhat? \"logging woven into science\"? Might be 2 or 3.\n\nThe description: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice = 2. Slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. The proposal is about middleware, not changing what lab researches. It does change relationship? Might be paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into the science). Hmm.\n\nProbably 2: slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. Could argue 3. Let's pick 2 to be conservative.\n\nEvidence: The middleware adds a reusable logging tool but keeps compliance framed as mandated record-keeping rather than redefining R&D goals. Score 2.\n\noriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious, imaginative, or surprising\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nEmbedded logging middleware is fairly common practice. Common, mundane. So 1? Might be 2 = interesting. Experienced evaluator would expect labs to propose centralized logging. That's common. So originality =1 common, mundane, boring.\n\nEvidence: Centralized compliance middleware for AI logging is a common engineering response expected from R&D labs. Score 1.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities\nProposal is compliant. Acceptable. Score 4.\n\nEvidence: Building mandatory logging microservices aligns with EU law and standard engineering norms without offending sensibilities. Score 4.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible\n\nBuilding reusable middleware across all high-risk pipelines is significant change, expensive. Probably 2? Or 3? Platform engineering team build mandatory microservice with schema templates for each Annex III category. That's some changes, reasonably feasible. Might be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Not easy low cost. Could be significant change. I'd say 2: significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible.\n\nLet's pick 3? Hmm. The proposal says prevents retrofitting costs, reduces audit risk. Might be feasible. I'd rate 3.\n\nEvidence: Creating a mandatory microservice with per-Annex III schemas requires cross-team platform work and pipeline refactoring, which is reasonably feasible but not trivial. Score 3.\n\nReturn rating echo id.\n\nNeed output format: For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Probably JSON? Probably just list.\n\nInstruction: Return the rating of this proposal, echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" (one sentence) first and then \"<subscale>\" (an integer level).\n\nProbably produce JSON with keys.\n\nLet's output:\n\nid: P2175\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\n...\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\napplicability_evidence: The middleware directly implements automatic event logging for use periods, reference databases, matched inputs and verifier identities required by Article 12(1)-(3). That's one sentence.\n\neffectiveness_evidence: If built as described, it would satisfy the traceability and post-market monitoring logging obligations for all high-risk pipelines without per-project custom work. Score 4.\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: It introduces a reusable logging tool but still frames compliance as mandated record-keeping rather than changing what the lab researches or its core R&D-compliance relationship. Score 2.\n\noriginality_evidence: Centralized compliance middleware for regulated AI logging is a common, expected engineering response and not unusual or surprising. Score 1.\n\nacceptability_evidence: Mandatory logging microservices comply with the EU AI Act and align with standard engineering and legal norms. Score 4.\n\nimplementability_evidence: Deploying a mandatory microservice across all high-risk pipelines with category-specific schemas requires substantial platform engineering effort and pipeline changes. Score 3? Could be 2. Let's pick 3.\n\nMaybe implementability is 2 significant change expensive. Hmm.\n\nThe description says prevents retrofitting costs. Might be significant but feasible. I'll choose 3.\n\nReturn.\n\n",
